  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 AM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nA strong upper-level low and surface low will pass well north of the \nQuad State Monday night through Wednesday. This will drag a \nnorth/south oriented cold front through the area from west to east \nTuesday into Wednesday morning Ensemble guidance continues to hint \nat a better chance of seeing substantial rainfall with around a 60-\n80% chance of the Quad State seeing an inch or more of rain through \nWednesday evening. PWAT values are forecast to be around 200-300% of \nnormal for this time of year and close to climatological maximum,\nso very heavy downpours are expected Tuesday afternoon into \nWednesday morning. \n\nGusty winds will likely develop from the south as the \npressure gradient tightens ahead of the approaching cold front \nduring the day Tuesday. In fact, there is around a 60-90% chance \nthan wind gusts exceed 30 mph across the area on Tuesday. Overall \ninstability looks to be fairly meager with the system with \nwidespread rainfall and cloud cover, so overall severe weather \nprobabilities remain low. Shear will be strong enough to support \ndamaging wind gusts if they can make it down to the surface. In \naddition, there is also a low-end tornado threat with 0-3km shear \nvectors maintaining at least a portion of 30-40kt shear \northogonal to the north/south line of showers/storms. The best \nchance would be late Tuesday morning into Tuesday afternoon. \nDefinitely supports a Marginal Risk highlighted by the SPC. \n\nConditions dry out for Thursday through the most of the weekend with \ntemperatures dropping down to seasonal norms. That would place highs \nin the low to mid 60s and lows in the 30s to around 40.",
